%!testlib
% What scope a spot function runs in on a device that halftones for
% itself, run through `xpost -d pbm` by run-screen-ps-test.sh.
%
% A screening device builds its threshold cell by sampling the spot
% function across the cell, and the builder holds its own working names
% in a dictionary of its own -- two of them, counting the refresh that
% calls it. The samples must not run under those. A program defines a
% counter through its spot and reads it back afterwards, and a
% definition landing in the builder's dictionary is thrown away with it;
% every name the builder holds shadows one of the program's meanwhile.
%
% The sampling on the setscreen path states this requirement above
% itself and compiles its calls to run after its dictionary has ended.
% This is the same requirement on the other path to the same procedure,
% and that path is reached only by painting on a device that screens --
% which is why this suite is painted somewhere that does, and why the
% ordinary null-device harness cannot ask the question at all.

/scr.depth 0 def
/scr.proc false def
/scr.ran false def
/scr.top 0 def

countdictstack /scr.top exch def

150 45 { pop pop
         /scr.ran true store
         countdictstack /scr.depth exch store
         currentdict /proc known /scr.proc exch store
         0.5 } setscreen
0 0 20 20 rectfill

scr.ran (the screening device sampled the spot function) assert
scr.depth scr.top eq
    (the spot function ran in the scope its caller had) assert
scr.proc not
    (the spot function did not see the builder's own names) assert

% PLRM 8.2 has setscreen "perform the equivalent of sethalftone", which it
% cannot do by taking what sethalftone will not. A frequency is cells to
% the inch, so at zero or below it names no screen, and a halftone
% dictionary stating one is already refused. What setscreen did instead
% was record the frequency and install nothing, so currenthalftone handed
% back a dictionary that its own sethalftone refused -- a program could
% not put back the screen it had just been given.
{ 0 0 { pop } setscreen } /rangecheck
    (a screen of no frequency) raises
{ -10 0 { pop } setscreen } /rangecheck
    (a screen of negative frequency) raises
60 0 { pop } setscreen
{ currenthalftone sethalftone } outcome /ok eq
    (the screen setscreen installs is one sethalftone takes back) assert

verdict
